From: BigFred hub-kernel <noreply@dcc-bigfred>
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] net: macb: IMR PCIe barrier after IER re-enable in macb_tx_poll

PR raspberrypi/linux#7340 patch 2 used a destructive ISR read after
re-enabling TCOMP. RP1 GEM is clear-on-read, so that consumed TCOMP.
raspberrypi/linux#7472 dropped it for that reason.

Keep the PCIe read barrier from #7369: IMR is the read-only mask
mirror, no side effects. Combined with the posted-write NCR flush
already in this pin (MACB_CAPS_PCIE_POSTED_WRITES on raspberrypi,rp1-gem),
macb_tx_complete_pending() can observe flushed TX_USED DMA.

Not-upstream: #7472 remains the last word on rpi-6.18.y.

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/cadence/macb_main.c
@@ -2010,6 +2010,12 @@
 		 * actions if an interrupt is raised just after enabling them,
 		 * but this should be harmless.
 		 */
+		/* IMR is the read-only mask mirror: no side effects on
+		 * clear-on-read or W1C ISR silicon.  The MMIO read is a
+		 * PCIe barrier so macb_tx_complete_pending() sees flushed
+		 * TX_USED DMA writes after TCOMP was masked.
+		 */
+		(void)queue_readl(queue, IMR);
 		if (macb_tx_complete_pending(queue)) {
 			queue_writel(queue, IDR, MACB_BIT(TCOMP));
 			if (bp->caps & MACB_CAPS_ISR_CLEAR_ON_WRITE)
